SCENARIO-You are developing an order history page. The orders table has order_id, customer_id,
restaurant_id, and total_amount. The restaurants table has restaurant_id and name.
The page must show order ID, restaurant name, and amount for every order — including
any that reference a restaurant no longer in the table.

Question: Compare INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN for this query. Which would you use and why?
Describe what each join type does to records that have no match in the other table.

Answer- 
Which Join to Use and Why?
we should use a LEFT JOIN for this query.

Left Join returns left table + common data.

Inner Join returns only common data.

Applying this to your tables (orders as the left table and restaurants as the right table):

How LEFT JOIN handles the scenario:
Your requirement states that the page must show order details "for every order — including any that reference a restaurant no longer in the table."
Since LEFT JOIN returns all records from the left table (orders) plus any matching records from the right table (restaurants), every single order 
will be displayed. If a restaurant is no longer in the table, the query will still include the order, returning NULL for the missing restaurant details.

Why INNER JOIN fails here:
An INNER JOIN only returns common data (matching records in both tables). If an order references a restaurant that has been deleted from the restaurants table, 
there is no match, so the INNER JOIN will drop that order entirely from your results, violating your requirement.
